    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d157">

                <pb facs="0314" n="210" sameAs="frus:frus1890/pg210"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Tsui</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1890-01-31"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 31,
                        1890</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of the 16th ultimo, in which, with reference to the announcement
                    made to you by the note of the Department dated 6th ultimo, touching the
                    amendment made by the Treasury of its circular No. 100 of September 28, 1889,
                    you say “it is understood that” the transportation companies engaged in the
                    business of conveying Chinese laborers in transit, “centering at New York
                    (through which the Chinese residents of Cuba principally pass), are unwilling to
                    give any bond for this traffic,” such as contemplated in the amendment
                    named.</p>
                <p>I am highly gratified to be able to inform you, however, that, as appears by a
                    letter of the Treasury Department of the 28th instant now before me, the
                    Southern Pacific Company, which is understood to control a large share of the
                    Chinese transit business, is about to execute the bond provided for in the
                    “amendment.” The exaction of the special bond of $200 in respect of each
                    laborer, so far as concerns those carried by that company, would in such case
                    cease.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">James G. Blaine.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
